The origin of the phrase "here be dragons" is uncertain, but one popular theory suggests that it comes from the Latin "hic sunt dracones," which means "here are dragons." This phrase was possibly added to maps by early cartographers to warn sailors and explorers of the dangers lurking in uncharted waters or unexplored lands.

However, it is important to note that many modern historians and scholars argue that the phrase "here be dragons" was more likely a later addition to maps, added by map collectors and enthusiasts rather than the original cartographers. They point out that few genuine medieval maps contain this phrase, and those that do often have questionable authenticity.

